\begin{remark}\label{rem:annulus-gate}
Theorem~\ref{thm:annulus-nogo} closes one possible shortcut:
\[ \boxed{\ \begin{array}{c}\text{inner-circle degree one}\\ +\ \text{analytic boundary extension}\\ +\ \text{holomorphic immersion}\\ +\ \text{Kahn's modulus relation}\end{array}\ \not\Longrightarrow\ \text{global injectivity}.\ } \]
Therefore the direct-decoration embedding gate cannot be solved by strengthening Kahn's inner-collar lemma through only its stated annular hypotheses. The actual Kahn--Lyubich question remains narrower: one must either (1) prove global univalence from additional dynamically generated path-space/fiber-product structure; (2) produce an actual KL base-annulus collision together with its lifted deck certificate; or (3) replace the full tile by a collar-truncated, sheet-sensitive carrier program.
\end{remark}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:psiql-nogo}
There is a degree-two \(\psi\)-polynomial-like map \((i,f):(\mathbf U',K')\to(\mathbf U,K)\) in the exact sense of Definition~\ref{def:psiql} such that:
\begin{enumerate}
\item \(\mathbf U'\) and \(\mathbf U\) are disks;
\item \(K\Subset\mathbf U\) is an FJ-set;
\item \(f:\mathbf U'\to\mathbf U\) is a proper holomorphic branched cover of degree two;
\item \(i:\mathbf U'\to\mathbf U\) is a holomorphic immersion;
\item the common inverse \(K'=i^{-1}(K)=f^{-1}(K)\) is connected;
\item \(i|_{K'}:K'\to K\) is a homeomorphism;
\item the complementary-annulus map \(i:\mathbf U'\setminus K'\to\mathbf U\setminus K\) induces \(+1\) on fundamental groups; but
\item \(i\) is not injective, even on \(\mathbf U'\setminus K'\).
\end{enumerate}
Consequently, neither the proper degree-two companion map nor the common connected full-continuum pullback axiom upgrades the immersion in an abstract \(\psi\)-quadratic-like pair to a global embedding.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:local-compat}
The pair \((i,f)\) of Theorem~\ref{thm:psiql-nogo} does not contradict Kahn's local-embedding theorem: \(i\) is injective on every set of imaginary diameter less than \(100\), and in particular on a sufficiently small neighborhood of \(K'=[-1,1]\). Hence the local polynomial-like restriction and the embedded inner annular collar of Kahn's theorem remain valid for this pair; what fails is precisely the unsupported passage from the local collar to the entire complementary annulus.
\end{proposition}

theorems.proof_latex:
\begin{proof}
By the exact collision criterion (B.7), every nontrivial collision \(i(z)=i(w)\), \(z\ne w\), has \(z-w=100ik\) with \(k\ne0\), hence imaginary displacement at least \(100\). Consequently \(i\) is injective on every set of imaginary diameter less than \(100\), including a sufficiently small neighborhood of \([-1,1]\). Kahn proves exactly a polynomial-like restriction: an embedding on a neighborhood selected near \(K'\), obtained by local injectivity near \(K'\) followed by truncation to an inner annular collar (\texttt{psiql2.tex}, statement at lines 60--77, proof at lines 155--178); no global embedding of \(\mathbf U'\) is asserted there, and Kahn--Lyubich import only the local embedding near \(K'\) (\texttt{psiql.tex}, lines 17--24). The present pair satisfies those local conclusions and refutes only the global upgrade.
\end{proof}

